        'The general solution is x(t) = A cos(ωt + φ), with two constants fixed by the initial position and velocity. Differentiating gives v = −Aω sin(ωt + φ), which leads x by a quarter cycle, and a = −ω²x, which is exactly opposite x.',
        'Adding damping gives m x″ + b x′ + kx = 0. For light damping the solution is a decaying sinusoid, x = A₀e^(−γt) cos(ω_d t + φ), with γ = b/2m and ω_d = √(ω₀² − γ²). When γ = ω₀ (b = 2√(mk)) the motion is critically damped.',
        'Many real oscillators — a pendulum at large amplitude, θ″ = −(g/L) sin θ — have no simple closed-form solution. Numerical methods step forward in small time intervals: from the current x and v, compute a, then update v ← v + aΔt and x ← x + vΔt (Euler–Cromer). Smaller steps, or the more accurate RK4 method, track the true solution closely.',
